@node	0.8, 0.9, 0.7, Chapter 0
@section Errors and tests

@code{Result<T, E>} (@ref{0.4}) is how a Rust function that can fail says so
in its type: @code{Ok(T)} on success, @code{Err(E)} on failure, checked by
the compiler at every call site. This book gives each edition exactly one
error enum, derived once with the @code{thiserror} crate so every variant
gets a @code{Display} message from a single attribute:

@example rust
use thiserror::Error;

#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Error)]
enum ArithError @{
    #[error("division by zero")]
    DivideByZero,
@}

fn safe_div(dividend: i128, divisor: i128) -> Result<i128, ArithError> @{
    if divisor == 0 @{
        Err(ArithError::DivideByZero)
    @} else @{
        Ok(dividend / divisor)
    @}
@}
@end example

Calling a function that returns @code{Result} and handling the error
yourself means a @code{match} on every call; the @code{?} operator collapses
that into one character where the caller's own function also returns a
compatible @code{Result}: @code{?} unwraps an @code{Ok} and continues, or
returns the @code{Err} immediately from the enclosing function.

@example rust
fn main() -> Result<(), ArithError> @{
    let quotient = safe_div(10, 2)?;
    println!("@{quotient@}");
    // => 5

    let failure = safe_div(1, 0);
    println!("@{failure:?@}");
    // => Err(DivideByZero)
    Ok(())
@}
@end example

This is the shape of every fallible function from chapter 1 on: the book's
one @code{SchemeError} enum plays the role @code{ArithError} plays here, and
@code{?} is how every interpreter and machine in this book propagates a
failure without hand-written checks cluttering the procedure that triggers
it. The runnable listing above is
@code{examples/sec_0_8_errors_and_tests.rs}.

@subheading Testing

@code{#[test]} marks a function as a test; @code{cargo nextest run} is this
book's test runner (doctests, the fenced code blocks inside doc comments,
run separately with @code{cargo test --doc}, since nextest does not execute
them). @code{assert_eq!} and @code{assert!} are the ordinary assertions, and
they are how this book proves a listing prints what it claims to print and an
exercise's solution computes what its statement promises.

Two more tools appear where a single hand-picked example is not enough
proof. @newterm{Property testing}, via the @code{proptest} crate, checks that
a stated property holds across many generated inputs instead of one:

@example rust
use proptest::prelude::*;

proptest! @{
    #[test]
    fn safe_div_agrees_with_ordinary_division(a in 1i128..1000, b in 1i128..1000) @{
        prop_assert_eq!(safe_div(a, b), Ok(a / b));
    @}
@}
@end example

@code{proptest!} generates hundreds of @code{(a, b)} pairs in the stated
ranges and runs the body against each; a failing case is automatically
@newterm{shrunk} to the smallest input that still fails before proptest
reports it, so the counterexample you see is close to minimal. This one
sketch is illustrative rather than a listing this book asserts against; the
edition's own property tests, checked exactly as shown, begin in chapter 1.

@newterm{Snapshot testing}, via the @code{insta} crate, pins a large or
generated output (an interpreter transcript, a rendered picture, a compiled
instruction sequence) against a saved reference file with @code{assert_snapshot!}
or @code{assert_debug_snapshot!}, and fails loudly if a change alters that
output unexpectedly; @code{cargo insta review} is how a genuine, intended
change gets accepted into a new reference file. Both tools appear from
chapter 1 on wherever a plain @code{assert_eq!} would be too narrow (one
example proves too little) or too verbose (the expected value is pages long)
to write by hand; this section is only the tour.

Every exercise in this book ships three files: a scaffold under
@code{exercises/} whose test is deliberately failing (more on this in
@ref{0.9}) until solved, a reference solution with a passing test under
@code{solutions/}, and that solution's reasoning in an adjacent Markdown
file. @code{cargo nextest run --workspace} runs every solved test in the
edition in one pass.

@subheading Exercise

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 0.5}Exercise 0.5:} Implement @code{sum_cubes(a, b)},
the sum @math{a^3 + (a+1)^3 + \cdots + b^3}, twice: once as a recursive
function, once as an iterator chain over @code{a..=b} (@ref{0.5}). The two
must agree. Then write a function that divides that sum by the count of
terms to get the mean cube, returning a @code{Result} from @code{safe_div}
and propagating it with @code{?}, from both the recursive and the iterative
sum.
@end quotation

The scaffold is @code{exercises/ch00/sec_0_8.rs}; the reference solution and
its reasoning are @code{solutions/ch00/sec_0_8.rs} and
@code{solutions/ch00/ex_0_05.md}.
